{"__type":"$$EventMessageAiNode","id":"b38ca470-5c92-4cdc-9a1e-58c90dffc13c","ts":"2025-08-19T15:27:04.420-04:00","eventName":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","message":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","payload":{"executionId":"25","nodeName":"OpenAI Chat Model","workflowName":"My workflow","nodeType":"@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.lmChatOpenAi","workflowId":"KzOup6x5YW2TVpKq","msg":"{\"messages\":[\"Human: From this PDF, can you tell me how to get started with the webhooks? \\nAI: \\nTool: []\"],\"options\":{\"openai_api_key\":{\"lc\":1,\"type\":\"secret\",\"id\":[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]},\"model\":\"gpt-5-mini\",\"timeout\":60000,\"max_retries\":2,\"configuration\":{\"baseURL\":\"https://api.openai.com/v1\"},\"model_kwargs\":{}},\"response\":{\"response\":{\"generations\":[[{\"text\":\"I don’t have the PDF you mentioned — could you upload it or paste the relevant pages or a link? If you do that I’ll extract the exact steps from the PDF. In the meantime, here’s a concise, practical “get started” guide for webhooks (generic + n8n-specific) so you can begin right away.\\n\\nGeneric webhook quick-start (steps)\\n1. Understand the flow\\n   - The sender (service A) makes an HTTP request to a URL you expose (your webhook endpoint) whenever an event happens.\\n   - Your endpoint receives the request, validates it, and processes or forwards the data.\\n\\n2. Create an endpoint to receive POSTs\\n   - Quick example (Node/Express):\\n     - npm install express body-parser\\n     - Code:\\n       const express = require('express');\\n       const app = express();\\n       app.use(express.json());\\n       app.post('/webhook', (req, res) => {\\n         console.log('payload', req.body);\\n         res.status(200).send('ok'); // respond quickly\\n       });\\n       app.listen(3000);\\n   - For local development, use ngrok (ngrok http 3000) to get a public URL.\\n\\n3. Test the webhook\\n   - Use curl or Postman:\\n     curl -X POST https://your-public-url/webhook -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" -d '{\\\"event\\\":\\\"test\\\"}'\\n   - Verify your app logs the payload and returns 200.\\n\\n4. Validate and secure\\n   - Use a secret header or signature (HMAC). Example: sender includes X-Signature = HMAC_SHA256(body, secret). Your endpoint verifies it.\\n   - Use HTTPS, restrict IPs if possible, and rate-limit.\\n\\n5. Acknowledge quickly, process asynchronously\\n   - Respond 200 quickly to avoid retries, queue heavy work (job queue, background worker).\\n\\n6. Handle retries & idempotency\\n   - Many services retry on non-2xx. Design your processing to be idempotent (use event IDs).\\n\\n7. Logging, monitoring, and retries\\n   - Log received events, response codes, and errors. Add alerts for failed processing.\\n\\nn8n-specific quick-start (if you're using n8n)\\n1. Create a new workflow.\\n2. Add a Webhook node (Core -> Webhook).\\n3. Configure:\\n   - HTTP Method (POST typically)\\n   - Path (e.g., my-webhook)\\n   - Response Mode (e.g., On Received, Last Node)\\n4. Save & execute the Webhook node (click “Execute Node” or enable workflow).\\n5. Copy the webhook URL shown by n8n:\\n   - If n8n runs at https://example.com, URL is https://example.com/webhook/my-webhook\\n   - If running locally, expose via ngrok and use that URL.\\n6. Send a test POST (curl or the external service). The Webhook node will capture the payload and you can see it in the node output.\\n7. Connect downstream nodes to process the payload (set, function, HTTP request, database, etc.).\\n8. Activate the workflow for production use. Make sure your n8n instance is reachable (HTTPS) or behind a tunnel.\\n\\nExample curl to test n8n webhook:\\ncurl -X POST \\\"https://your-n8n-url/webhook/my-webhook\\\" -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" -d '{\\\"hello\\\":\\\"world\\\"}'\\n\\nCommon pitfalls\\n- Not exposing the endpoint publicly for external services (use ngrok or host publicly).\\n- Slow responses causing retries; be quick to respond and process later.\\n- Not verifying payload authenticity — leads to spoofing.\\n- Forgetting to activate the workflow (n8n) or save changes.\\n\\nIf you want, upload the PDF or tell me which PDF (file name or link) and I’ll extract the exact “webhooks” section and give step-by-step instructions shown there.\",\"generationInfo\":{\"prompt\":0,\"completion\":0,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"system_fingerprint\":null,\"model_name\":\"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07\"}}]]},\"tokenUsage\":{\"completionTokens\":1268,\"promptTokens\":176,\"totalTokens\":1444}}}"}}
