sten permanently.\\n5. Get the webhook URL:\\n   - Copy it from the Webhook node UI (or from the temporary URL shown during Execute). If you run n8n behind a reverse proxy, the public URL uses the instance’s configured webhook base URL.\\n6. Send a request from the external service to that webhook URL. Example curl:\\n   curl -X POST 'https://your-n8n-url/webhook/<path>' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\\\"foo\\\":\\\"bar\\\"}'\\n7. View incoming data and executions in the n8n Executions panel and adjust nodes as needed.\\n\\nSecurity and deployment notes\\n- If running n8n locally, use a tunnel (ngrok, localtunnel) to expose a public URL for testing.\\n- Use Webhook node Authentication (Basic, Header, Query) or implement signature checking in a Function node (e.g., verify an HMAC header) for stronger security.\\n- If you need stable public URLs in production, set the webhook base URL in n8n config (so links in nodes are generated correctly) and put n8n behind HTTPS reverse proxy.\\n\\nIf you want, upload the PDF now and I’ll extract the exact instructions from it and summarize any special options or examples that are in that document.\",\"generationInfo\":{\"prompt\":0,\"completion\":0,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"system_fingerprint\":null,\"model_name\":\"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07\"}}]]},\"tokenUsage\":{\"completionTokens\":1587,\"promptTokens\":184,\"totalTokens\":1771}}}"}}
