{"__type":"$$EventMessageAiNode","id":"e7ee5fbd-6b07-436f-b197-fc9c76981d06","ts":"2025-08-19T15:37:06.728-04:00","eventName":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","message":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","payload":{"executionId":"31","nodeName":"OpenAI Chat Model","workflowName":"My workflow","nodeType":"@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.lmChatOpenAi","workflowId":"KzOup6x5YW2TVpKq","msg":"{\"messages\":[\"Human: Explain to me what is RAG? \"],\"options\":{\"openai_api_key\":{\"lc\":1,\"type\":\"secret\",\"id\":[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]},\"model\":\"gpt-5-mini\",\"timeout\":60000,\"max_retries\":2,\"configuration\":{\"baseURL\":\"https://api.openai.com/v1\"},\"model_kwargs\":{}},\"response\":{\"response\":{\"generations\":[[{\"text\":\"RAG = Retrieval‑Augmented Generation. It’s an approach that combines a retrieval system (search over documents) with a generative large language model (LLM) so the LLM can answer questions and produce text grounded in external knowledge. Instead of relying only on the model’s internal parameters (which can be outdated or hallucinate), RAG “pulls in” relevant facts from a document collection and uses them as context for generation.\\n\\nCore idea / pipeline\\n- Document store: a collection of knowledge (PDFs, web pages, DB rows, transcripts).\\n- Indexing/embedding: split documents into chunks and index them (sparse methods like BM25 or dense vector embeddings + ANN).\\n- Retrieval: given a user query, retrieve the top-k most relevant chunks.\\n- Generation (reader): pass the retrieved chunks + query to a generative model which composes a final answer. The model conditions on retrieved content to ground its response.\\n- (Optional) Reranking, citation: rerank candidates and include provenance (source links) in the output.\\n\\nCommon architectures\\n- Retrieve-then-Generate: retrieve relevant passages and prepend/append them to the prompt for the LLM.\\n- Fusion-in-Decoder (FiD): encode each retrieved passage separately and fuse them in the decoder (used for more structured fusing).\\n- Retrieve-and-Rerank: retrieve many, rerank with a stronger model, then generate.\\n- Augmented Search: combine sparse + dense retrieval (hybrid) to improve recall.\\n\\nBenefits\\n- Up-to-date / dynamic: can use current data without retraining the LLM.\\n- More factual / reduced hallucination (if retrieval is high-quality).\\n- Smaller LLMs can match performance of larger ones by relying on retrieved facts.\\n- Traceability: can cite source passages for provenance.\\n\\nLimitations / risks\\n- If retrieval returns irrelevant or wrong passages, the LLM can still hallucinate or produce incorrect syntheses.\\n- Latency: retrieval + LLM inference adds runtime.\\n- Index staleness: the document store must be kept updated.\\n- Privacy/security: sensitive data must be protected, and the LLM must not leak it.\\n\\nPractical tips / best practices\\n- Chunk size: pick chunk sizes that fit prompt/context window and preserve coherent passage meaning (e.g., 200–1,000 tokens depending on use).\\n- Use embeddings + ANN for semantic retrieval for better recall, consider hybrid with BM25.\\n- Return top‑k (commonly 3–20) and experiment with k; too many can overwhelm prompt/context.\\n- Rerank retrieved results with a cross-encoder if precision is critical.\\n- Provide provenance in answers (source id, excerpt, link).\\n- Prompt engineering: instruct the LLM to only use retrieved info or say when it’s guessing.\\n- Monitoring: evaluate factuality, retrieval quality, and latency. Keep the index up to date.\\n\\nWhere it’s used\\n- Document Q&A (enterprise knowledge bases, legal, medical notes)\\n- Conversational assistants grounded in company docs\\n- Customer support automation\\n- Code search + synthesis from internal repos\\n- Any scenario requiring up‑to‑date, explainable answers from large corpora\\n\\nTools / frameworks (examples)\\n- LangChain, LlamaIndex, Haystack, Elasticsearch + custom embeddings, FAISS, Milvus, Weaviate.\\n\\nSimple example flow\\n1) User asks: “What’s the refund policy for orders placed in Europe?”\\n2) Retriever finds three policy paragraphs from a company handbook.\\n3) LLM receives the query + those 3 paragraphs, then generates: a concise answer and cites the paragraphs it used.\\n\\nIf you want, I can:\\n- Show a short code example (Python) using embeddings + an LLM.\\n- Walk through architecture choices (vector store, ANN index, reranker).\\n- Give a troubleshooting checklist for common RAG problems. Which would you like?\",\"generationInfo\":{\"prompt\":0,\"completion\":0,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"system_fingerprint\":null,\"model_name\":\"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07\"}}]]},\"tokenUsage\":{\"completionTokens\":1125,\"promptTokens\":139,\"totalTokens\":1264}}}"}}
