{"__type":"$$EventMessageAiNode","id":"a37359ec-18c3-4576-8048-40cea085c608","ts":"2025-08-19T15:38:47.127-04:00","eventName":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","message":"n8n.ai.llm.generated","payload":{"executionId":"32","nodeName":"OpenAI Chat Model","workflowName":"My workflow","nodeType":"@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.lmChatOpenAi","workflowId":"KzOup6x5YW2TVpKq","msg":"{\"messages\":[\"Human: What can the agent do differently than a human being? \\nAI: \\nTool: []\"],\"options\":{\"openai_api_key\":{\"lc\":1,\"type\":\"secret\",\"id\":[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]},\"model\":\"gpt-5-mini\",\"timeout\":60000,\"max_retries\":2,\"configuration\":{\"baseURL\":\"https://api.openai.com/v1\"},\"model_kwargs\":{}},\"response\":{\"response\":{\"generations\":[[{\"text\":\"I’m not sure which kind of “agent” you mean (software/AI agent, RAG agent, chatbot, robot, etc.), so I’ll answer broadly. Below are the main ways an automated agent typically behaves differently from a human — strengths, weaknesses, and practical examples — and a short note on how to combine them effectively.\\n\\nWhat an agent can do differently (strengths)\\n- Scale and parallelism: process and act on millions of items or many users at once (e.g., index+search huge document sets, personalize recommendations for millions).\\n- Speed: perform computations, searches, and repetitive operations orders of magnitude faster than humans.\\n- Availability and endurance: run 24/7 without fatigue, sleep, or breaks.\\n- Consistency and reproducibility: apply the same rules or model behavior consistently across time and users.\\n- Large-context data processing: ingest, store, and retrieve far more data (logs, documents) than a human can remember.\\n- Automation of repetitive tasks: execute routine workflows reliably (e.g., data entry, scheduled reporting, email routing).\\n- Multimodal integration and API control: query databases, call web APIs, run jobs, and control systems programmatically.\\n- Massive pattern detection: find subtle statistical correlations across huge datasets that humans would miss.\\n- Auditing and logging: keep precise, searchable records of decisions and actions for compliance.\\n- Cost-efficient scaling: after deployment, handling more volume typically costs less than hiring many humans.\\n\\nWhere agents typically differ in a limiting way (weaknesses)\\n- Common sense and deep context: struggle with implicit context, real-world nuance, and long-term causal reasoning in novel situations.\\n- Judgment, ethics, and values: lack true moral reasoning, empathy, and social understanding; they follow rules or learned patterns, not conscience.\\n- Creativity and insight: can assist creative work but usually don’t originate deep, genuinely novel ideas the way humans do.\\n- Robustness in the real world: physical robots still lag humans in dexterity, perception, and handling unexpected environments.\\n- Hallucinations and errors: AI models can produce plausible but incorrect outputs; they don’t “know” when they’re wrong unless explicitly checked.\\n- Accountability and legal responsibility: agents cannot take legal or moral responsibility — humans or organizations must.\\n- Trust and rapport: humans communicate empathy, negotiate, and build trust in ways agents can’t fully replicate.\\n\\nConcrete examples\\n- Better for agents: continuous monitoring of infrastructure, instant search across millions of documents, auto-classifying and routing support tickets, running repeated simulations, enforcing business-rule checks, or performing high-speed financial trades.\\n- Better for humans: resolving complex customer disputes, making ambiguous ethical decisions, creative strategy, high-level planning across uncertain domains, or delicate negotiations.\\n\\nBest practice: human-in-the-loop\\n- Use agents to do scale, routine, and data-heavy tasks and to surface options or summaries.\\n- Keep humans for final judgment, ethical decisions, ambiguous cases, and creative work.\\n- Put guardrails: verifications, explainability, logging, and escalation paths for when the agent is uncertain.\\n\\nIf you meant a specific agent type (a RAG agent, chatbot, robotic agent, or something else), tell me which one and I’ll give a tailored comparison and concrete examples.\",\"generationInfo\":{\"prompt\":0,\"completion\":0,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"system_fingerprint\":null,\"model_name\":\"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07\"}}]]},\"tokenUsage\":{\"completionTokens\":989,\"promptTokens\":168,\"totalTokens\":1157}}}"}}
